schools are the ones with the lowest school IDs in that file.
}
\details{
\strong{Design.} The five schools (IDs 1224, 1288, 1296, 1308 and 1317) have
47, 25, 48, 20 and 48 students. Schools 1224, 1288 and 1296 are public;
schools 1308 and 1317 are Catholic. Because \code{size} and \code{sector} are
constant within a school, they vary across only five schools: a sector
comparison rests on three public and two Catholic schools, and a model
with school dummies, \code{factor(schoolid)}, cannot separate them from the
school effects (R reports the aliased terms as \code{NA}). Students in the same
school are not independent, so ordinary least-squares standard errors,
especially for school-level predictors, can be too small.

\strong{Coding.} \code{minority}, \code{female} and \code{sector} are 0/1 dummies (\code{sector}
1 = Catholic). There are no missing values, and version 1.0.0 changed no
values.

\strong{Suggested analyses.}
\itemize{
\item simple and multiple regression of \code{mathach} on \code{ses}, \code{minority},
\code{female} and \code{sector};
\item interactions, e.g. whether the \code{ses} slope differs by sector
(\code{ses * sector});
\item model building and regression diagnostics in a small sample;
\item a first look at nested data: school dummies, \code{factor(schoolid)}, or a
separate \code{ses} slope in each school.
